WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.006 --> 00:00:03.313
[SOUND] Hear that?

00:00:03.313 --> 00:00:05.310
Silence.

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:09.852
We often equate communication
with talking, but not talking,

00:00:09.852 --> 00:00:14.840
that is listening is as
important if not more important.

00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:16.011
I'm Matt Abraham, and

00:00:16.011 --> 00:00:20.270
I teach Strategic Communication at
Stanford Graduate School of Business.

00:00:20.270 --> 00:00:24.860
Welcome to Think Fast Talk Smart,
The Podcast, today I am so

00:00:24.860 --> 00:00:30.353
excited to be joined by my strategic
communication teaching partner and

00:00:30.353 --> 00:00:33.870
longtime friend Kristin Hanson.

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:38.753
Beyond teaching our class, Kristin
recently co-taught leadership for society,

00:00:38.753 --> 00:00:41.780
big arguments courageous leadership.

00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:46.057
Beyond her lecturing at the GSB,
Kristin is the executive director of

00:00:46.057 --> 00:00:51.260
the Civic Health Project and a board
member of the Listen First Project.

00:00:51.260 --> 00:00:55.470
Hey there partner, welcome to the podcast,
ready to have some fun?

00:00:55.470 --> 00:00:59.070
&gt;&gt; Well, Matt, I always have fun
talking and teaching with you.

00:00:59.070 --> 00:01:03.624
In fact, there's nothing I'd rather be
doing every Monday before 8 o'clock in

00:01:03.624 --> 00:01:04.746
the morning [LAUGH].

00:01:04.746 --> 00:01:08.161
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] It is quite early,
there are lots of things I would rather

00:01:08.161 --> 00:01:09.328
do before 8,
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:01:09.328 --> 00:01:11.680
&gt;&gt; But I truly enjoy teaching with you

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:12.853
&gt;&gt; Let's go ahead and

00:01:12.853 --> 00:01:16.663
get started, we have known each other for
a long, long time,

00:01:16.663 --> 00:01:21.577
we taught our first public speaking
class together in Stanford's engineering

00:01:21.577 --> 00:01:24.681
school while undergraduates
three decades ago.

00:01:24.681 --> 00:01:27.619
&gt;&gt; Well as they say Matt,
time flies when you're having fun.

00:01:27.619 --> 00:01:31.090
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] And things get better
with age at least I hope.

00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:34.767
Back then we had a lot of fun helping
our students communicate better,

00:01:34.767 --> 00:01:35.514
what are one or

00:01:35.514 --> 00:01:40.860
two best practices you find yourself still
highlighting after all of these years?

00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:45.559
&gt;&gt; Our challenge back at that time was
helping students in technical degree

00:01:45.559 --> 00:01:49.830
programs to convey complex
ideas in more accessible ways.

00:01:49.830 --> 00:01:54.581
And to this day, that is something I
still really enjoy and I know you do too,

00:01:54.581 --> 00:01:58.739
helping students craft engaging
storylines from dense complex or

00:01:58.739 --> 00:02:01.160
highly technical material.

00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:06.635
And, I also remembered we helped
students reduce filler words like and

00:02:06.635 --> 00:02:09.980
like, from their verbal communications.

00:02:09.980 --> 00:02:14.241
We would actually bang a book on
the desk each time they did it, well,

00:02:14.241 --> 00:02:16.830
we don't bank books anymore, do we?

00:02:16.830 --> 00:02:19.861
But we still help students
to be more confident and

00:02:19.861 --> 00:02:22.470
fluid in their verbal delivery.

00:02:22.470 --> 00:02:26.198
&gt;&gt; I remember banging books,
ringing bells, clapping hands, and today,

00:02:26.198 --> 00:02:28.860
of course there's an app for that.

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:31.635
One thing many folks don't know about you,

00:02:31.635 --> 00:02:35.322
is that you were a true pioneer
in virtual communication.

00:02:35.322 --> 00:02:40.227
Our students really [LAUGH] enjoy and get
a kick out of showing the video we have of

00:02:40.227 --> 00:02:45.080
you selling the first video conference
technologies from the last century.

00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:50.053
&gt;&gt; Okay Matt [LAUGH], now you're really
making me feel old, but you're right,

00:02:50.053 --> 00:02:54.049
I actually worked for
the early 1990's version of Zoom.

00:02:54.049 --> 00:02:58.425
&gt;&gt; Thinking back to then and bringing
it forward to now, what are a couple of

00:02:58.425 --> 00:03:03.280
the best practices you can share about
hybrid in virtual communication?

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:06.895
&gt;&gt; Effective virtual communication both,
then and now is so

00:03:06.895 --> 00:03:12.200
much about getting the setup right, is my
network connection strong and reliable?

00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:13.630
How's my lighting?

00:03:13.630 --> 00:03:15.450
Should I use headphones with a mic?

00:03:15.450 --> 00:03:17.750
Pro tip, yes, you should.

00:03:17.750 --> 00:03:21.110
Do I have permission from the other
side to display my slides?

00:03:21.110 --> 00:03:22.839
Getting any of these things wrong,

00:03:22.839 --> 00:03:26.670
risks losing your audience before
you've even gotten underway.

00:03:26.670 --> 00:03:31.210
And once the show begins, it's about
finding ways to leap out of your screen.

00:03:31.210 --> 00:03:35.123
How do I let my audience know that
not only do they see me, I see them,

00:03:35.123 --> 00:03:39.044
effective virtual communication
is about closing the distance and

00:03:39.044 --> 00:03:42.710
coming across a bigger than that
little screen that holds you.

00:03:43.880 --> 00:03:47.670
&gt;&gt; So many important points there
about presence, about engagement,

00:03:47.670 --> 00:03:49.160
about permission.

00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:53.696
But the one thing I heard you say that I
really want to dig deep into that helped

00:03:53.696 --> 00:03:58.240
me so much when we went virtual
was the notion of it's a show.

00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:01.080
You helped me understand
that you have to plan and

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:03.860
coordinate your virtual communication.

00:04:03.860 --> 00:04:06.548
You have to think about the timing,
who speaks when,

00:04:06.548 --> 00:04:09.116
how quickly do you move from
one thing to the other,

00:04:09.116 --> 00:04:12.690
just like you're an executive
producer of a television show.

00:04:12.690 --> 00:04:14.796
And once I started doing that,

00:04:14.796 --> 00:04:19.100
it freed me up to be more of myself
in my virtual communication.

00:04:19.100 --> 00:04:21.664
So thank you for that personally,
and thank you for

00:04:21.664 --> 00:04:24.190
the advice you shared with all of us.

00:04:24.190 --> 00:04:28.692
I'd like now to shift gears from talking
and presenting which is what we primarily

00:04:28.692 --> 00:04:32.281
help our students with here at
the business school to listening,

00:04:32.281 --> 00:04:36.330
which is a big focus of your work
outside of the Business School.

00:04:36.330 --> 00:04:38.148
Can you describe that work and

00:04:38.148 --> 00:04:42.250
how it relates to the principle
of listening first?

00:04:42.250 --> 00:04:46.280
&gt;&gt; Well, after getting my MBA here at
Stanford and enjoying a long career in

00:04:46.280 --> 00:04:50.903
the tech sector, I started worrying a few
years ago and I know I'm not alone, about

00:04:50.903 --> 00:04:55.870
what seemed like an alarming deterioration
in our civil discourse here in the US.

00:04:55.870 --> 00:04:57.909
Infecting our politics or media,

00:04:57.909 --> 00:05:03.070
even just everyday interactions
among families, friends, neighbors.

00:05:03.070 --> 00:05:06.818
And for me that sense of alarm
prompted me to pivot out of tech and

00:05:06.818 --> 00:05:11.561
into an emerging sector in our country
full of organizations that are focused on

00:05:11.561 --> 00:05:16.810
helping Americans to bridge our social,
cultural and political divides.

00:05:16.810 --> 00:05:20.479
So now, I run a philanthropy it's
called Civic Health Project, and

00:05:20.479 --> 00:05:24.730
we help organizations that are doing
bridge building work across the US.

00:05:24.730 --> 00:05:29.621
And I also serve on the Board of Listen
First Project, hashtag listen first.

00:05:29.621 --> 00:05:31.760
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].
&gt;&gt; The umbrella organization that

00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:33.203
represents hundreds,

00:05:33.203 --> 00:05:39.490
literally hundreds of grassroots bridge
building organizations across the country.

00:05:39.490 --> 00:05:43.090
Collectively this whole field is
focused on a pretty monumental task,

00:05:43.090 --> 00:05:46.330
we're trying to shift norms in
our country towards listening.

00:05:46.330 --> 00:05:50.341
And away from cancelling toward
intellectual humility and

00:05:50.341 --> 00:05:54.672
away from absolutism and
virtue signaling toward curiosity and

00:05:54.672 --> 00:05:59.112
away from animosity
&gt;&gt; I still respect the efforts that you

00:05:59.112 --> 00:06:03.604
and your colleagues are doing to really
help make a difference in our civil

00:06:03.604 --> 00:06:05.124
discourse and I can see so

00:06:05.124 --> 00:06:09.053
clearly how you can apply the skills
that we teach our students.

00:06:09.053 --> 00:06:13.837
I'm curious if we can bring this back
to a very tactical set of advice and

00:06:13.837 --> 00:06:17.827
guidance, what are some best
practices you've learned,

00:06:17.827 --> 00:06:22.725
that leaders, managers, and
employees can use to help listen better?

00:06:22.725 --> 00:06:25.321
&gt;&gt; Well,
there's no silver bullet here, but

00:06:25.321 --> 00:06:30.640
it's important to understand that
better listening starts with intent.

00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:35.984
Listening actively and deeply happens when
I genuinely believe that the person who's

00:06:35.984 --> 00:06:40.904
speaking, has intrinsic worth and brings
a perspective that I lack and I need.

00:06:40.904 --> 00:06:46.262
In work settings just like in our personal
lives, our relationships thrive when

00:06:46.262 --> 00:06:53.020
we cultivate our own open mindedness,
intellectual humility, genuine curiosity.

00:06:53.020 --> 00:06:56.766
I love how the book
Conscious Leadership by John Mackey and

00:06:56.766 --> 00:07:02.090
Steve McIntosh explores this idea of
cultivating an integral worldview.

00:07:02.090 --> 00:07:04.240
Urging business leaders to appreciate and

00:07:04.240 --> 00:07:07.346
synthesize employees' diverse
backgrounds and beliefs and

00:07:07.346 --> 00:07:11.900
perspectives when aiming to resolve
conflict or make transformative change.

00:07:11.900 --> 00:07:17.281
Active listening is at the core of
developing this more integral worldview.

00:07:17.281 --> 00:07:22.127
And when we listen it isn't a passive
exercise ,active inquiry is part of

00:07:22.127 --> 00:07:27.520
listening to, the trick is asking genuine
questions that open up new pathways for

00:07:27.520 --> 00:07:28.706
understanding.

00:07:28.706 --> 00:07:33.833
Now this can really feel hard, when the
viewpoints or the values that are being

00:07:33.833 --> 00:07:38.972
expressed by somebody else challenge
our own, including in our workplaces.

00:07:38.972 --> 00:07:43.896
But that is also when the greatest
opportunity for learning presents itself,

00:07:43.896 --> 00:07:47.562
even if we understand this intuitively,
it is hard to do.

00:07:47.562 --> 00:07:51.284
&gt;&gt; Practicing listening just like
practicing speaking is so critical,

00:07:51.284 --> 00:07:54.185
one of the key elements that
you talked about Kristin,

00:07:54.185 --> 00:07:57.880
was this notion of how you approach it,
it's your mindset.

00:07:57.880 --> 00:08:02.158
Many of us listen just enough to try
to understand what the person is saying

00:08:02.158 --> 00:08:07.100
rather than really deeply try to
figure out the nuance, the meaning.

00:08:07.100 --> 00:08:11.041
I like to instruct people to listen
by trying to figure out what's

00:08:11.041 --> 00:08:14.280
the bottom line of what
the person is saying.

00:08:14.280 --> 00:08:18.760
Now, I have to say, I might be better at
teaching this than actually doing it.

00:08:18.760 --> 00:08:22.152
In the conversations we have
sometimes I don't listen well and

00:08:22.152 --> 00:08:25.928
my wife reminds me all the time that
I don't listen as well as I can, but

00:08:25.928 --> 00:08:28.070
we need to listen with intent.

00:08:28.070 --> 00:08:31.572
And then I would really like what
you said about inquiry following,

00:08:31.572 --> 00:08:35.074
so it's not just about listening,
it's about synthesizing, and

00:08:35.074 --> 00:08:38.041
then following up through listening and
paraphrasing.

00:08:38.041 --> 00:08:42.587
Last fall, you co-taught with Brian Lowry,
a former guest here,

00:08:42.587 --> 00:08:48.840
a class called leadership for society,
big arguments, courageous leadership.

00:08:48.840 --> 00:08:53.500
What was the main thrust of that course
and what takeaways can you share with us?

00:08:53.500 --> 00:08:57.026
&gt;&gt; Co-teaching that course big
arguments courageous leadership with

00:08:57.026 --> 00:09:00.623
Dean Lowry was a chance to equip
students here at the business school.

00:09:00.623 --> 00:09:05.295
With some of the theories out of social
psychology as well as the tools and

00:09:05.295 --> 00:09:10.353
practices, to help listen better,
contemplate different perspectives and

00:09:10.353 --> 00:09:12.971
navigate conflicts where they emerge.

00:09:12.971 --> 00:09:18.552
From the boardroom to the school board to
thanksgiving weekend with family [LAUGH].

00:09:18.552 --> 00:09:20.987
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; One of the main concepts that we urged

00:09:20.987 --> 00:09:23.049
our students to contemplate and

00:09:23.049 --> 00:09:27.510
focus on is developing and
applying that integral worldview.

00:09:27.510 --> 00:09:32.281
That considers and synthesizes different
perspectives among diverse workforces and

00:09:32.281 --> 00:09:33.136
citizenries.

00:09:33.136 --> 00:09:36.287
And just to broaden that
a bit beyond workplaces and

00:09:36.287 --> 00:09:41.341
leadership roles that many of our students
will go on to inhabit in the workplace,

00:09:41.341 --> 00:09:44.510
just looking at our society,
as Americans today.

00:09:44.510 --> 00:09:47.904
By now we can all see that the shouting,
the posturing, the grandstand,

00:09:47.904 --> 00:09:50.160
isn't really getting us anywhere.

00:09:50.160 --> 00:09:55.048
One of my favorite quotes from a colleague
Mark Curzon is the world today

00:09:55.048 --> 00:09:59.020
has an advocacy surplus and
an inquiry deficit.

00:09:59.020 --> 00:10:01.746
These massive societal
challenges that we're facing,

00:10:01.746 --> 00:10:05.559
they're not going to get solved if we just
sort and retreat into tribal factions.

00:10:05.559 --> 00:10:08.257
We label Americans outside
our own tribes as enemies,

00:10:08.257 --> 00:10:12.180
we lose any sense of trust or
goodwill towards one another.

00:10:12.180 --> 00:10:17.759
Social Science Research, and this is a lot
of what we delved into in the course,

00:10:17.759 --> 00:10:20.922
it makes it clear that
when we engage in deep,

00:10:20.922 --> 00:10:25.789
genuine listening, perspective taking and
perspective sharing.

00:10:25.789 --> 00:10:29.364
So that is hearing other
people's lived experiences and

00:10:29.364 --> 00:10:33.790
honestly sharing our own,
that's how we get down to the root.

00:10:33.790 --> 00:10:38.423
That's how we begin to cultivate deep
reservoirs of trust, goodwill and

00:10:38.423 --> 00:10:43.204
empathy toward one another, and that
is a recipe for success in business and

00:10:43.204 --> 00:10:44.417
in our civic life.

00:10:44.417 --> 00:10:46.594
&gt;&gt; So much there that is so important,

00:10:46.594 --> 00:10:51.250
the foundation of this podcast is really
based on some of those principles.

00:10:51.250 --> 00:10:57.244
It's really better understanding yourself,
being able to listen and then respond and

00:10:57.244 --> 00:11:02.428
communicate in a way that is connected
respectful and hopefully positively

00:11:02.428 --> 00:11:07.450
directed towards solving problems,
achieving goals, pitching well.

00:11:08.870 --> 00:11:11.331
Given the wide variety
of topics you teach,

00:11:11.331 --> 00:11:14.003
do you have any last ideas
you'd like to share?

00:11:14.003 --> 00:11:18.916
&gt;&gt; Here's my chance to plug the idea
that business can actually be uniquely

00:11:18.916 --> 00:11:24.070
impactful in helping to shift people
in our society and other societies away

00:11:24.070 --> 00:11:29.002
from deep division and toward greater
social cohesion through skills.

00:11:29.002 --> 00:11:33.415
That include, listening, inquiry,
perspective sharing, and so

00:11:33.415 --> 00:11:36.050
on and there and three reasons for this.

00:11:36.050 --> 00:11:40.754
First of all, we know that business
leaders are more highly trusted

00:11:40.754 --> 00:11:45.060
than nearly all other institutional
leaders in our society.

00:11:45.060 --> 00:11:49.350
So there's receptivity to the idea
that business leadership can

00:11:49.350 --> 00:11:50.916
point the way forward.

00:11:50.916 --> 00:11:54.395
Second, workforces are used
to getting trained and

00:11:54.395 --> 00:11:59.848
acquiring new skills at work, it's one of
the few places that we achieve norms and

00:11:59.848 --> 00:12:04.300
skill development and
at scale after we become adults.

00:12:04.300 --> 00:12:07.785
And finally, work is one place
where we as Americans do tend

00:12:07.785 --> 00:12:12.455
to bump up against people with different
backgrounds, beliefs, and values,

00:12:12.455 --> 00:12:16.910
something that's becoming more rare
in all other parts of our lives.

00:12:16.910 --> 00:12:22.938
So it's a perfect place not only to learn
but also to practice skills of listening,

00:12:22.938 --> 00:12:26.656
bridging, problem solving and
social cohesion.

00:12:26.656 --> 00:12:31.786
&gt;&gt; So well put, it is abundantly clear
that the skills we teach can be so

00:12:31.786 --> 00:12:36.736
helpful to our students,
to their colleagues that they will have

00:12:36.736 --> 00:12:41.460
in the future in the workforce,
and to our society.

00:12:41.460 --> 00:12:45.880
I really, really appreciate you
highlighting the value of listening and

00:12:45.880 --> 00:12:48.590
strategic communication.

00:12:48.590 --> 00:12:52.912
Now, I've been looking forward to this for
a long time Kristin, before we end,

00:12:52.912 --> 00:12:56.855
I'd like to ask you the same three
questions I ask everybody who joins me,

00:12:56.855 --> 00:12:57.951
are you up for that?

00:12:57.951 --> 00:12:58.979
&gt;&gt; I am.

00:12:58.979 --> 00:13:02.514
&gt;&gt; If you were to capture
the best communication advice

00:13:02.514 --> 00:13:07.428
you ever received as a five to seven
words slide title, what would it be?

00:13:07.428 --> 00:13:12.359
&gt;&gt; Use a microphone and hold it close to
your mouth, sorry, that's nine words.

00:13:12.359 --> 00:13:16.208
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] So,
I know you're big on amplifying your

00:13:16.208 --> 00:13:17.131
message,
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:13:17.131 --> 00:13:18.062
&gt;&gt; I'm curious,

00:13:18.062 --> 00:13:23.000
why of all the things you could have
said did you choose to say that?

00:13:23.000 --> 00:13:27.953
&gt;&gt; It's the simplest thing, but if your
audience can't hear you they're going to

00:13:27.953 --> 00:13:31.240
tune out and
you don't want that to happen.

00:13:31.240 --> 00:13:33.305
&gt;&gt; And I know you have so
many suggestions for

00:13:33.305 --> 00:13:37.494
how to get your audience's attention to
sustain that attention and you're right,

00:13:37.494 --> 00:13:40.570
if they can't hear you they
can't listen and learn.

00:13:40.570 --> 00:13:41.910
Question number two,

00:13:41.910 --> 00:13:47.470
what are the first three ingredients that
go into a successful communication recipe?

00:13:47.470 --> 00:13:52.708
&gt;&gt; I'd be remiss if I didn't share the
advise we give our students quarter after

00:13:52.708 --> 00:13:58.470
quarter, know your audience, know your
context, and know your speaking goal.

00:13:58.470 --> 00:14:02.488
What is it you want that audience
to leave, thinking, feeling and

00:14:02.488 --> 00:14:07.030
doing differently because
they've just listened to you?

00:14:07.030 --> 00:14:11.877
&gt;&gt; There you go, folks, you don't have to
attend our class, you got it all in three

00:14:11.877 --> 00:14:16.170
key ideas, know your audience,
know your context and know your goal.

00:14:17.250 --> 00:14:19.870
Who is a communicator that you admire and
why?

00:14:21.500 --> 00:14:26.330
&gt;&gt; Well Matt, in saying this I'm ending
our talk on a somewhat more serious note.

00:14:26.330 --> 00:14:31.140
But my answer to this question has to be
Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelensky,

00:14:31.140 --> 00:14:35.463
talk about knowing your audience,
your context and your speaking goal,

00:14:35.463 --> 00:14:38.140
I mean well, literally under fire.

00:14:38.140 --> 00:14:39.489
He has the presence and

00:14:39.489 --> 00:14:43.962
the composure to explain what he needs
whether from the Ukrainian people,

00:14:43.962 --> 00:14:49.140
from Russia or from Western Democracies,
I'm flared, amazed and humbled.

00:14:49.140 --> 00:14:55.256
&gt;&gt; Absolutely, it's been truly remarkable
to see somebody under the pressures and

00:14:55.256 --> 00:15:00.850
stresses that are going on to communicate
so clearly and so passionately.

00:15:02.740 --> 00:15:05.340
Kristin, thank you so much for being here,

00:15:05.340 --> 00:15:09.207
you know I love collaborating with you and
learning from you, and

00:15:09.207 --> 00:15:13.100
I love the fact that our audience
gets to learn from you as well.

00:15:13.100 --> 00:15:16.425
Thanks for sharing your insights and
ideas with all of us and

00:15:16.425 --> 00:15:20.565
thanks for not sharing any of the
